  "scope": [
    "The condition sets are declared by the atom/relator catalog and propagated structurally. They are not extracted from opaque Lean proof terms or arbitrary host programs and are not claimed semantically minimal.",
    "The changed condition IDs and proof transfer outside them are explicit inputs. There is no automatic semantic diff of arbitrary propositions or models; the updated conditional atom/relator realizers must themselves be verified.",
    "Pure layout and relator catalog stay fixed during repair. Updating games, kernels, conditions or prices is allowed through a checked updated Model/HigherModel. This proves the new proof, not equality of all old and new endpoint games.",
    "Relators must preserve symbolic charges. Kernel-equal paths with different chosen error ledgers need a separate error-improvement rule; general approximate game paths are directed and have no inverse execution constructor.",
    "The earlier complete exchange-fragment basis is not automatically a complete basis for this conditional probabilistic syntax. No global mixed-cryptographic coherence, copy/discard completion or finite concrete generator claim is made.",
    "The MAC order is the prior coordinate/prepared-set construction order of an already sampled secret function. It does not exchange the adaptive attacker queries. Original attack, state, trace and native caller implementations remain unchanged.",
    "The MAC example preserves the existing 1/256 upper bound and is not a new cryptanalytic bound. One fresh condition among two declared IDs is not a proof-byte, DAG-size, checking-time or exclusive higher-dimensional advantage.",
    "The counterexamples distinguish reuse of one existing diagram from availability of another proof. Rejecting that diagram is not claiming the outer endpoint statement is false.",
    "Native CPU time, computational assumptions requiring certified reductions, automatic discovery of local relators, complete repair minimality and fair shared-proof baseline cost comparisons remain outstanding."
  ],
